abstract: >-
  An open, machine-readable dataset of complexity pairs (computational problems for which two or more correct
  algorithms of different asymptotic cost are recorded) and of proved theorem notes. Every entry carries an explicit
  verification level enforced by the included validator: V1 for implementations that agree with each other on a
  test battery (and with an independent oracle where the harness defines one), and V2 for measured growth (an exact
  operation count, or wall-clock time where no count is used yet) that fits the claimed cost and rejects declared
  rival costs; recorded runs also store wall-clock time next to every exact count. Items marked Proved carry the project's own written proof of every claim and deterministic
  checks that anyone can replay with one command (tools/replay_proofs.py); every check exits non-zero on failure.
  Provenance labels mark own results, own extensions, and results whose provenance is undetermined because a source
  that might contain them could not be read; those sources are listed. Results, refutations, corrections and null
  results are documented in a research log, and recorded validation runs store every measured value.
  Nearly all of the code, entries and documentation were written by an AI system (Claude, by Anthropic) under
  the author's direction; the author is responsible for the content, and the README section "How this project
  is made" describes the roles.
